// Lesson 1.1 — Launch: Fork, Clone & Press Play
// Day one: every student forks the starter, clones it, opens it in Unity, and
// presses Play. The world is drawn and the three scenes are wired to load one
// another, but there is no game logic anywhere — the starter PlayerController
// switches the walking animation on in Start() and does nothing else.
// Students build every mechanic themselves.
//
// STRUCTURE (tightening pass): no scaffold level and no walkthrough — there is
// no code to write on day one — but the two halves of a lesson still travel
// together, so the clicks (fork, clone, open, Play, commit) are the "In the
// Editor" list and the file tree you just forked is "In the Code".
//
// CULLED FOR REPETITION AND PRE-EXPLANATION: the "Why so empty?" callout
// explained the whole semester's method (read the docs, cut behaviour into
// parts) six lessons before any of it happens — that is 5.1's and 6.4's job,
// and the third Socratic question already asks it. The old "Tour" activity
// restated the editor steps from 1.2, the "Ship It" activity restated the
// commit step, and the "why so empty" ul of future topics pre-explained
// lessons 1.4 to 2.4.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted, so the socratic keeps its two
// committed questions untouched and the third is appended at the end.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.1 — Launch: Fork, Clone &amp; Press Play</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">Today you get the world you will bring to life this semester. The sprites are drawn, the tilemaps are laid out, and your character already breathes an idle animation. Press Play and hold a direction key, though, and <strong>nothing happens</strong>: every script in the project is a shell with blank methods in it, and filling them in is the work of the year.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span><span class="activity-time">~25 min</span></div>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>GitHub →</strong> open the <code>cs-curriculum-main</code> link your teacher posts in Canvas, sign in, and click <strong>Fork</strong> (top right). A fork is a copy of the repo that belongs to <em>you</em> — commits go to your copy, so you cannot break the original.</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub →</strong> green <strong>Code</strong> button, copy the HTTPS URL. Then <strong>GitHub Desktop →</strong> File → Clone Repository → URL tab → paste it → <strong>Clone</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Unity Hub → Add →</strong> pick the cloned <code>cs-curriculum-main</code> folder and open it. The first open takes a while — Unity is importing every sprite and sound in the project.</li>
        <li><strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/_Scenes</code>. Three scenes, already wired to load one another: <strong>Start</strong> (the title screen), <strong>Overworld</strong> (where you play) and <strong>Platformer</strong> (the cave).</li>
        <li><strong>Hierarchy →</strong> open <strong>Overworld</strong> and select the <strong>Player</strong>. <strong>Scene view →</strong> that is your character, standing in the middle of the map.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> press <strong>Play</strong> and hold WASD or the arrow keys. The idle animation plays and the character does not move an inch — everything you can see is drawn, and nothing is wired up.</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub Desktop →</strong> commit with the message <code>"1.1: cloned the starter"</code>, then push. Every class this year ends here, and your commit history becomes the record of what you built.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Code</span><span class="activity-time">~15 min</span></div>
    <p>No code to write today. Go and read the tree you just forked — that third column is what you are starting from.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Folder</th><th>What lives there</th><th>Current state</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/Scripts</code></td><td>PlayerController, EnemyController, TurretController, Projectile, StatManager, Lever</td><td><strong>Shells.</strong> One line of behaviour in the whole folder: PlayerController switches the walking animation on once, at the start. Nothing in the project reacts to anything.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts</code></td><td>CameraController, SceneSwitch, TopDown_EnemyAnimator</td><td><strong>The only working code you inherit.</strong> The camera follows you and a cave door loads a scene. That is all of it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/Prefabs</code></td><td>Player, Coin, Spikes, MobileEnemy, Turret, AxeItem, Switch</td><td>Pictures with tags. No physics, no behaviour.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/_Scenes</code></td><td><code>Start</code> (title screen), <code>Overworld</code>, <code>Platformer</code></td><td>Built, saved, and wired to load each other.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/Artwork</code></td><td>Top-down tiles, cave tilesets, characters, weapons</td><td>Finished art, ready to use.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your character plays an idle animation without a single line of your code. Who or what is driving that animation right now?</li>
            <li>Why is building a game system yourself from documentation more useful for learning computer science than reading a finished project and answering questions about what each line does?</li>
            <li>Every script you just forked is a class with blank methods. What does that tell you about the difference between a file that exists and a program that runs?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain the difference between a fork and a clone</li>
        <li>Open the Overworld scene in Unity and enter Play mode</li>
        <li>Say what your character does on Play, and confirm it cannot move yet</li>
        <li>Find <code>Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs</code> in the Project window, and say what it currently does</li>
        <li>Commit and push your work to your own GitHub repository</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the foundational tools:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — the editor tour, scripts, input, and vectors</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — every API this course uses</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="1.2.html">1.2 — The Unity Editor Tour</a>, where you learn to read the tool that runs all of this.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.1" title="1.1 Launch: Fork, Clone &amp; Press Play — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
